@meta
id: 04-03
title: Give the motion a number
chapter: 04
type: lesson
skills: hjkl, count-prefix
par: 20
minutes: 4
vscode: page down, then hunt with the mouse
open: payments.log
vimrc: set number

@brief
Sixty lines of payment log. One charge was declined and the retry flag
never got written. It is on line 27, and it is the only line in the file
that does not end in `ok`.

Append `  retry` — two spaces, then the word — to the end of that line.

You could hold `j` down, or the down arrow, and watch the cursor drift
past it twenty-six lines later. Do not. Every
motion in vim takes a number in front of it: type the number first, then
the motion, and the motion happens that many times. `10j` is ten lines
down, as one thought and three keystrokes, and it lands exactly where you
said rather than wherever you let go.

Line numbers are switched on for this file so you can do the arithmetic.

@keys
| `26j` | down twenty-six lines |
| `9k` | up nine lines |
| `3l` | right three characters |
| `A` | append at the end of the line |

@hints
You start on line 1 and you want line 27. The count is the difference,
not the destination.
--
`26j`. Type the digits first, then `j` — vim collects the number and
waits for a motion to spend it on.
--
`26j` then `A`, type the two spaces and `retry`, `Esc`, `:wq`.

@solution
    26j A <space><space>retry <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Fifteen keystrokes for a jump that would have been twenty-six taps, or a
held key and a correction afterwards.

Two things are worth taking from this. The first is that a count is
universal: it works in front of `h`, `j`, `k`, `l` and in front of nearly
every other motion in the rest of this course, so learning it once pays
out forever.

The second is the arithmetic you just did. You wanted line 27, you were on
line 1, so you worked out 26. That subtraction is a small tax on every
jump, and you will pay it on every single one until lesson six, where a
single setting does the sum for you.

